// Module 9: rates of flux change and circuit energy.
qualitative(9,'Zero flux does not mean zero emf',2,'A rigid loop rotates at constant angular speed in a uniform magnetic field. At one instant its magnetic flux is zero. Can the induced emf magnitude be nonzero at that instant?',[
 ['Yes. For sinusoidal flux, its magnitude is maximum when flux crosses zero.','Emf depends on the rate of flux change, not the instantaneous flux alone.'],['No. Zero flux always gives zero emf.','This confuses a value with its derivative.'],['Only if the magnetic field magnitude increases.','Changing loop orientation can change flux even when the field magnitude is constant.'],['No. A rigid loop cannot have changing flux.','The loop area can stay fixed while its angle changes.']],
 [E('Write the flux',r`\Phi=BA\cos(\omega t)`),E('Differentiate it',r`\mathcal E=-N\frac{d\Phi}{dt}=NBA\omega\sin(\omega t)`),E('Check the zero-flux orientation',r`\cos(\omega t)=0\ \Rightarrow\ |\sin(\omega t)|=1`),T('Keep the distinction','The field, area, or orientation can change the flux.')]);
qualitative(9,'Lenz direction for a weakening field',3,'A conducting loop lies in the page. A uniform external magnetic field points out of the page and decreases in magnitude. Viewed from the front, which way does the induced conventional current flow?',[
 ['Counterclockwise.','Its field points out of the page and opposes the decrease of outward flux.'],['Clockwise.','That produces an inward field and reinforces the loss of outward flux.'],['There is no current because the field does not reverse.','A changing magnitude is enough to induce emf.'],['The direction cannot be found without the resistance.','Positive resistance affects magnitude, not the Lenz-law direction.']],
 [T('Identify the change','Outward flux is decreasing.'),T('Oppose that change','The induced field must point outward.'),T('Apply the loop right-hand rule','An outward thumb gives counterclockwise curled fingers.'),T('Check the wording','The induced field opposes the change, rather than always opposing the external field.')]);
qualitative(9,'The same initial and final flux',2,'The magnetic flux through a fixed N-turn coil changes and later returns to its initial value. What follows for the time integral of its induced emf over the entire interval?',[
 [r`It is zero: $\int\mathcal E\,dt=0$.`,'The integral depends on the net flux change, which is zero.'],['The emf is zero at every instant.','A zero integral does not require a zero integrand at every instant.'],['The integral must be positive.','Positive and negative contributions can cancel.'],['The coil cannot exchange energy during the interval.','A zero emf integral alone does not determine electrical energy transfer.']],
 [E('Start from Faraday’s law',r`\mathcal E=-N\,d\Phi/dt`),E('Integrate with a fixed orientation',r`\int_{t_i}^{t_f}\mathcal E\,dt=-N(\Phi_f-\Phi_i)`),E('Use the equal endpoint values',r`\Phi_f=\Phi_i\ \Rightarrow\ \int\mathcal E\,dt=0`),T('Avoid an extra conclusion','The emf can be nonzero during parts of the interval.')]);
qualitative(9,'A transformer after the DC transient',5,'A transformer primary has nonzero winding resistance. A DC source has been connected long enough for its current and core flux to become constant. Ignore leakage and saturation. What is the secondary induced emf now?',[
 ['Zero.','The steady core flux has zero time derivative.'],['It follows the ordinary AC turns ratio with the DC source voltage.','The AC turns-ratio calculation requires changing linked flux.'],['It increases continuously with time.','The question states the flux is constant.'],['It is nonzero because the core flux itself is nonzero.','Flux magnitude alone does not determine induced emf.']],
 [T('Identify the stated steady condition','After the transient, the current and flux are constant.'),E('Apply Faraday’s law to the secondary',r`\mathcal E_s=-N_s\,d\Phi/dt`),E('Use the zero derivative',r`d\Phi/dt=0\ \Rightarrow\ \mathcal E_s=0`),T('Separate switching from steady operation','Connection and disconnection can produce transient secondary emf.')]);
qualitative(9,'An inductor starts to discharge',7,'An inductor carrying current is disconnected from its source and connected into a closed loop with a finite resistor. Which statement is correct immediately after switching in the ideal RL model?',[
 ['Current starts from its previous value and then decays continuously.','A finite inductor voltage permits a finite rate of current change.'],['Current instantly becomes zero.','That would require an impulse of voltage across the ideal inductor.'],['Current reverses instantly to oppose the old current.','Lenz’s law opposes the decrease of current, not the preexisting current itself.'],['The inductor creates energy to keep the current constant forever.','The initial magnetic energy becomes resistor heat.']],
 [E('Use the inductor relation',r`V_L=L\,di/dt`),E('Write the source-free loop equation',r`L\,di/dt+Ri=0`),E('Use current continuity',r`i(t)=I_0e^{-Rt/L},\quad i(0^+)=I_0`),T('Check energy transfer','The resistor dissipates the magnetic energy initially stored in the inductor.')]);
numerical(9,'Both field and area change',2,'A 100-turn coil has a uniform perpendicular field B(t) = (0.20 + 0.10t) T and area A(t) = (0.030 − 0.002t) m², with t in seconds. Choose positive flux along the field and positive emf around its right-hand-rule contour. Find the signed induced emf at t = 2.0 s.',-.18,r`\mathrm V`,[-.26,.08,.18],['This differentiates only the field and holds the changing area fixed.','This uses only the area-change contribution and its sign.','This misses Faraday’s minus sign for the chosen contour.'],[
 E('Use flux per turn',r`\Phi(t)=B(t)A(t)`),E('Differentiate both changing factors',r`\mathcal E=-N[A\,dB/dt+B\,dA/dt]`),E('Evaluate the instantaneous quantities',r`B(2)=0.40\,\mathrm T,\quad A(2)=0.026\,\mathrm{m^2}`),E('Substitute both contributions',r`\mathcal E=-100[(0.026)(0.10)+(0.40)(-0.002)]=-0.180\,\mathrm V`),T('Interpret the sign','Field growth dominates area shrinkage, so positive flux increases and the emf acts in the negative contour direction.')],{type:'flux-product',N:100,B0:.2,Bdot:.1,A0:.03,Adot:-.002,t:2});
numerical(9,'Emf of a rotating coil',2,'A 100-turn coil of area 0.010 m² rotates in a uniform 0.20 T field at 50 rad/s. Its normal makes an increasing angle of 30° with the field. Find the instantaneous induced emf magnitude.',5,r`\mathrm V`,[10,10*Math.cos(Math.PI/6),.1],['This gives the peak emf, not the value at 30°.','This uses cosine for the derivative of the flux.','This omits the angular speed factor.'],[
 E('Write the angle-dependent flux',r`\Phi=BA\cos\theta`),E('Differentiate using angular speed',r`|\mathcal E|=NBA\omega|\sin\theta|`),E('Substitute',r`|\mathcal E|=(100)(0.20)(0.010)(50)\sin30^\circ=5.00\,\mathrm V`),T('Check the angle','Use the angle to the normal. The maximum occurs at a normal angle of 90°.')],{type:'rotating-emf',N:100,B:.2,A:.01,omega:50,theta:30});
numerical(9,'Mechanical power in a sliding rod',4,'A 0.40 m conducting rod slides at constant 3.0 m/s on ideal rails in a uniform 0.50 T field. The rod, velocity, and field are mutually perpendicular. The closed circuit has total resistance 2.0 Ω. Ignore friction and self-inductance. Find the external mechanical power needed.',.18,r`\mathrm W`,[.6,.06,.36],['This is the numerical emf, not the mechanical power.','This is the numerical drag-force magnitude, not power.','This misses the resistance when calculating power.'],[
 E('Find motional emf',r`\mathcal E=BLv=(0.50)(0.40)(3.0)=0.60\,\mathrm V`),E('Find the current',r`I=\mathcal E/R=0.60/2.0=0.30\,\mathrm A`),E('Find magnetic drag and balance it',r`F_{\mathrm{ext}}=BIL=(0.50)(0.30)(0.40)=0.060\,\mathrm N`),E('Calculate mechanical power',r`P=F_{\mathrm{ext}}v=(0.060)(3.0)=0.180\,\mathrm W`),E('Check electrical heating',r`I^2R=(0.30)^2(2.0)=0.180\,\mathrm W`)],{type:'rod-power',B:.5,L:.4,v:3,R:2});
numerical(9,'An RL circuit reaches a chosen current',7,'A 12 V ideal source is connected to a series 6.0 Ω resistor and 3.0 H ideal inductor. The initial current is zero. Find the time when current first reaches 75% of its final value.',.5*Math.log(4),r`\mathrm s`,[.5,.5*Math.log(4/3),1.5],['One time constant gives about 63.2% of the final current.','This confuses the remaining fraction with the reached fraction.','A linear current-rise model does not apply.'],[
 E('Calculate the final current and time constant',r`I_\infty=12/6=2.0\,\mathrm A,\quad\tau=L/R=3/6=0.50\,\mathrm s`),E('Use the growth solution',r`i/I_\infty=1-e^{-t/\tau}`),E('Isolate the remaining fraction',r`e^{-t/\tau}=1-0.75=0.25`),E('Take logarithms',r`t=-0.50\ln0.25=0.50\ln4`),T('Check the time','Reaching 75% takes more than one time constant.')],{type:'rl-target',L:3,R:6,fraction:.75});
numerical(9,'Transformer input current under load',5,'An ideal transformer receives 24 V rms at its 100-turn primary. Its 500-turn secondary supplies a 240 Ω resistor. Use sinusoidal steady operation. Find the primary rms current.',2.5,r`\mathrm A`,[.5,.1,12.5],['This is the secondary current.','This applies the load directly to the primary voltage.','This applies the turns ratio twice to the primary current.'],[
 E('Find the secondary rms voltage',r`V_s=V_pN_s/N_p=24(500/100)=120\,\mathrm V`),E('Find secondary current',r`I_s=V_s/R=120/240=0.50\,\mathrm A`),E('Use ideal power conservation',r`V_pI_p=V_sI_s`),E('Solve the primary current',r`I_p=(120)(0.50)/24=2.50\,\mathrm A`),E('Check power',r`P_{\mathrm{in}}=24(2.50)=60\,\mathrm W=P_{\mathrm{load}}`)],{type:'transformer-current',Vp:24,Np:100,Ns:500,R:240});

// Module 10: boundaries, angle conventions, and photon energy.
qualitative(10,'What stays constant during refraction?',1,'Monochromatic light crosses a stationary boundary from air into ordinary transparent glass. Which quantities remain unchanged across the boundary?',[
 ['Frequency and photon energy.','The boundary preserves frequency, so photon energy hf remains the same.'],['Speed and wavelength.','Both usually decrease when light enters a higher-index medium.'],['Wavelength and photon energy.','Wavelength changes because speed changes while frequency stays fixed.'],['Frequency and speed.','The phase speed depends on the medium.']],
 [E('Use the wave relation',r`v=f\lambda`),E('Use the refractive-index definition',r`v=c/n`),E('Keep frequency fixed at the stationary boundary',r`\lambda_2/\lambda_1=v_2/v_1=n_1/n_2`),E('Check photon energy',r`E_\gamma=hf`)]);
qualitative(10,'Which angle belongs in Snell’s law?',4,'A ray in air makes an angle of 20° with a flat glass surface. Which incident angle belongs in Snell’s law?',[
 [r`$70^\circ$`,'Snell’s law uses the angle to the normal, complementary to the stated surface angle.'],[r`$20^\circ$`,'This is the angle to the surface, not its normal.'],[r`$40^\circ$`,'Doubling the surface angle does not give the incident angle.'],[r`$110^\circ$`,'The incident angle is the acute angle to the appropriate normal.']],
 [T('Draw the normal','Draw a line perpendicular to the interface.'),E('Convert the angle',r`\theta_i=90^\circ-20^\circ=70^\circ`),E('Use the normal-based law',r`n_1\sin\theta_i=n_2\sin\theta_t`),T('Keep a consistent convention','Measure reflected and transmitted angles from their interface normals too.')]);
qualitative(10,'At and above the critical angle',6,'A ray travels from a higher-index medium into a lower-index medium. What happens at the critical angle, in the usual ideal ray model?',[
 ['The refracted ray runs along the boundary at 90° to the normal.','Total internal reflection occurs for incident angles above the critical angle.'],['The incident angle is 90°.','The transmitted angle is 90° at the critical condition.'],['The ray bends to the normal.','High-to-low refraction bends away from the normal.'],['The same critical angle applies from low index to high index.','The high-to-low condition is necessary for this critical-angle relation.']],
 [E('Start from Snell’s law',r`n_1\sin\theta_i=n_2\sin\theta_t`),E('Set the limiting transmitted angle',r`\theta_t=90^\circ`),E('Solve the critical angle',r`\sin\theta_c=n_2/n_1\quad(n_1>n_2)`),T('Check the boundary case','Above this incident angle, Snell’s law gives no propagating transmitted ray.')]);
qualitative(10,'Parallel layers and a missing exit ray',5,'Light crosses parallel transparent layers. You calculate the final angle using n_initial sin θ_initial = n_final sin θ_final and obtain sin θ_final > 1. What is the correct interpretation?',[
 ['There is no transmitted exit ray through that limiting interface; check total internal reflection.','An inverse-sine argument above one is not a real ray angle.'],['Round the sine to one and report 90°.','Clamping changes the physical conditions and hides total internal reflection.'],['Ignore the intermediate layers and always report an angle.','The telescoped relation is conditional on transmission at each boundary.'],['Use the absolute value of the inverse-sine argument.','The argument already exceeds its allowed magnitude.']],
 [E('Keep the parallel-boundary invariant',r`n\sin\theta=\text{constant}`),T('Check each interface','A real transmitted angle requires the sine magnitude to be at most one.'),E('Test the high-to-low limit',r`\theta_i>\sin^{-1}(n_2/n_1)\ \Rightarrow\ \text{total internal reflection}`),T('State the limitation','The direct initial-to-final formula does not guarantee that an exit ray exists.')]);
qualitative(10,'Intensity below the photoelectric threshold',9,'In the ordinary one-photon photoelectric model, monochromatic light has photon energy below a metal’s work function. Its intensity doubles while frequency stays fixed. What happens?',[
 ['No photoemission occurs in this model.','More sub-threshold photons do not increase the energy of each photon.'],['The maximum electron kinetic energy doubles.','Maximum kinetic energy depends on photon frequency and work function.'],['Emission starts because total incident energy per second doubles.','The one-photon threshold concerns the energy of an individual absorbed photon.'],['The work function becomes half as large.','Intensity does not change the specified work function in this model.']],
 [E('Calculate energy per photon',r`E_\gamma=hf`),E('Apply the threshold condition',r`hf<\phi`),T('Separate intensity and frequency','At fixed frequency, increased intensity increases photon arrival rate.'),T('Apply the stated model','The photons still lack the energy needed to release an electron. Multiphoton processes are outside this question.')]);
numerical(10,'Wavelength inside water',1,'Light with vacuum wavelength 400 nm enters water with refractive index 4/3. The boundary is stationary. Find its wavelength in water.',300,r`\mathrm{nm}`,[400,1600/3,225],['This keeps wavelength fixed although phase speed changes.','This multiplies by the refractive index instead of dividing.','This applies the index reduction twice.'],[
 E('Keep the frequency fixed',r`f=c/\lambda_0`),E('Use phase speed in the medium',r`v=c/n`),E('Combine the relations',r`\lambda=v/f=\lambda_0/n`),E('Substitute',r`\lambda=400/(4/3)=300\,\mathrm{nm}`),T('Check energy','The frequency and photon energy stay the same across this stationary boundary.')],{type:'medium-wavelength',lambda0:400,n:4/3});
const refracted=Math.asin(Math.sin(Math.PI/3)/1.5)*180/Math.PI;
numerical(10,'Refraction from a surface angle',4,'A ray in air (n = 1.00) strikes glass (n = 1.50) at 30° to the surface. Find the transmitted angle to the normal.',refracted,r`{}^\circ`,[Math.asin(Math.sin(Math.PI/6)/1.5)*180/Math.PI,60,90-refracted],['This treats the surface angle as the incident normal angle.','This ignores refraction.','This gives the transmitted surface angle, not the requested normal angle.'],[
 E('Convert the incident angle',r`\theta_1=90^\circ-30^\circ=60^\circ`),E('Apply Snell’s law',r`(1.00)\sin60^\circ=(1.50)\sin\theta_2`),E('Solve the sine first',r`\sin\theta_2=\sin60^\circ/1.50=0.577350`),E('Take the inverse sine',r`\theta_2=\sin^{-1}(0.577350)`),T('Check the direction','The transmitted angle is less than 60°, so the ray bends toward the normal in glass.')],{type:'surface-refraction',surface:30,n1:1,n2:1.5});
const exitAngle=Math.asin(4/3*Math.sin(40*Math.PI/180))*180/Math.PI;
numerical(10,'An exit ray through three media',5,'A ray in water (n = 4/3) crosses glass (n = 1.50) and then air (n = 1.00). Both boundaries are parallel. The water angle is 40° to the normal. Find the final air angle to the normal. Check that transmission is possible.',exitAngle,r`{}^\circ`,[40,Math.asin(4/3/1.5*Math.sin(40*Math.PI/180))*180/Math.PI,Math.asin(Math.sin(40*Math.PI/180)/1.5)*180/Math.PI],['This assumes equal starting and ending indices.','This is the intermediate glass angle.','This ignores the initial water index and reports a glass-like angle.'],[
 E('Find the parallel-layer invariant',r`n_w\sin\theta_w=\frac43\sin40^\circ=0.857050`),E('Check the glass angle',r`\sin\theta_g=0.857050/1.50=0.571367`),E('Check the final interface',r`\sin\theta_a=0.857050/1.00<1`),E('Calculate the exit angle',r`\theta_a=\sin^{-1}(0.857050)`),T('Check total internal reflection','The glass-to-air interface transmits here. A larger starting angle can prevent an exit ray.')],{type:'parallel-exit',n1:4/3,n2:1.5,n3:1,theta:40});
const stopV=h*c/(350e-9*e)-2.2;
numerical(10,'Photoelectric stopping potential',9,'Light of wavelength 350 nm strikes a metal with work function 2.20 eV. Find the stopping-potential magnitude in the ordinary one-photon model. Use h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s, and e = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.',stopV,r`\mathrm V`,[stopV+2.2,2.2,stopV+4.4],['This is photon energy in eV before subtracting the work function.','This uses the work function alone.','This adds the work function instead of subtracting it.'],[
 E('Convert wavelength',r`\lambda=350\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm m`),E('Calculate photon energy in electronvolts',r`E_\gamma(\mathrm{eV})=\frac{hc}{\lambda e}=3.54521\,\mathrm{eV}`),E('Check threshold and subtract the work function',r`K_{\max}=3.54521-2.20=1.34521\,\mathrm{eV}>0`),E('Use the stopping relation',r`eV_{\mathrm{stop}}=K_{\max}`),T('Check the units','An electron kinetic energy of 1.34521 eV corresponds to a stopping-potential magnitude of 1.34521 V.')],{type:'stopping-potential',h,c,e,lambda:350e-9,phiEV:2.2});
numerical(10,'Find the critical angle from wave speed',6,'Light travels at 2.00 × 10⁸ m/s inside a transparent medium. It approaches a boundary with air (n = 1.00) from inside. Use vacuum c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s. Find the critical angle to the normal.',Math.asin(2/3)*180/Math.PI,r`{}^\circ`,[48.189685,Math.asin(1/1.5**2)*180/Math.PI,90],['This is the complementary angle to the surface.','This squares the refractive index without justification.','The transmitted angle, not the critical incident angle, is 90°.'],[
 E('Infer the refractive index',r`n_1=c/v=(3.00\times10^8)/(2.00\times10^8)=1.50`),E('Check the high-to-low condition',r`n_1=1.50>n_2=1.00`),E('Use the critical-angle relation',r`\sin\theta_c=n_2/n_1=2/3`),E('Calculate the normal angle',r`\theta_c=\sin^{-1}(2/3)`),T('Check the limiting ray','At this angle, the transmitted ray is along the boundary. Larger incident angles give total internal reflection in the ideal ray model.')],{type:'critical-from-speed',v:2e8,c,n2:1});

